course: dictionary
lesson: 13648
title: Reading between the lines
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sometimes a text does not say what a character feels or what has happened. You have to infer it. To infer means to work something out from clues in the text and what you already know.
explain: «His fists were clenched and his jaw was tight.» The text does not say, Tom is angry. But clenched fists and a tight jaw are clues, and you know that people often look like this when they are angry. So you infer that he is angry.
explain: Infer is a verb: I infer, she infers, they inferred. The noun is inference, which is the answer you reach. When you infer, ask yourself two questions: what clues can I see, and what do I already know?
fact: To infer means to work out something that is not said directly, using clues.
fact: Inference is the noun: it is the conclusion you reach from the clues.
fact: «His fists were clenched» shows you can infer that he is angry.
fact: In science, you infer from evidence: wet ground is a clue that it may have rained.
fact: Useful phrases: «infer from the text» and «what can you infer?»
exercise: «His fists were clenched and his jaw was tight.» What can you infer? | He is calm and relaxed | *He is angry | He is asleep | He is hungry
exercise: In science, Ava sees wet ground and says it must have rained. What is she doing? | Copying the answer from a book | *Inferring from evidence | Measuring the rain | Forgetting the question
exercise: Which word is the noun for infer? | infers | inferred | *inference | inferring
test: «Tom kept checking the clock and tapping his foot.» What can you infer about Tom? | *He is impatient and waiting for something | He is painting a picture | He has just won a prize | He is asleep
test: «The dishes were piled high and the room smelled of burnt toast.» What can you infer? | Everyone is very tidy | The kitchen has just been painted | *Nobody has cleaned up after breakfast | The family has just come back from a holiday
exam: Which sentence is an inference, rather than a fact stated directly? | Rain is wet | *The grass is wet, so it must have rained | The sky is blue today | Today is Monday
